    "author_association": "NONE",
    "source_id": "issue:3585",
    "repository": "streamlit/streamlit",
    "body": "### Summary\r\n\r\nUsing the new `session_state` functionality, when adding a function to the `on_click` attribute of `st.form_submit_button` (tested with sidebar), the callback is called the first time application runs before the user clicking.\r\n \r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\nCode snippet:\r\n\r\n```\r\ndef callback():\r\n  print(\"Foo\")\r\n\r\nwith st.sidebar:\r\n  with st.form(key='form'):\r\n    textbox = st.selectbox('select one', ['shao khan', 'sindel', 'the kollector'])\r\n    submit = st.form_submit_button(\"submit\", on_click=callback)\r\n\r\n```\r\n\r\n**Expected behavior vs Actual:**\r\n\r\nThe first time app runs there shouldn't be a \"Foo\" in the terminal and it should appear when form button is clicked. However it appears when the first time app is ran, without a click happening from the user.\r\n\r\n### Is this a regression?\r\n\r\nThat is, did this use to work the way you expected in the past?\r\nSession state is new functionality, so not applicable\r\n\r\n### Debug info\r\n\r\n- Streamlit version: 0.84.1\r\n- Python version: 3.7\r\n- Using Conda? Yes\r\n- OS version: Win 10\r\n- Browser version: Chrome (latest)",
